That’s the follow-on to “You’re the only one I trust to handle this.” The compliment can get you to accept responsibility before anyone has said what authority, time, or tools come with it.

“You can handle it” can be true in principle. You can know exactly how to get a job done and still be short two people, a week, or the authority to make the call. If the job fails, “you couldn’t handle it” turns those missing pieces into a verdict on you. “You were unequipped for what we asked you to do” gives everybody something concrete to examine.

And unequipped can include a skill you still need to develop. You might need practice setting a boundary, reading a room, or making a decision with incomplete information. Naming the missing skill gives you somewhere to put the work.

I’d put this right after the paragraph about taking the job if the hours, authority, and compensation match the responsibility:

“Can you handle it?” asks you to prove something about yourself. “What would it take to handle it well?” asks you to describe the job. You can trust your ability and still ask for what the work requires. “I can own the outcome if I have the authority to make that call” is an account of what it would take to deliver.